      "claim": "Thiacloprid, a close neonicotinoid analogue sharing the chloropyridylmethyl and cyclic guanidine-like core, showed direct brain penetration in mice after intraperitoneal dosing, with brain levels of 11 ppm at 15 min and measurable levels up to 240 min.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "Acetamiprid, another chloropyridyl neonicotinoid, was detected in rat brain along with its metabolites after systemic treatment, demonstrating that this chemical class can cross the blood-brain barrier in vivo.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is imidacloprid, a chloropyridyl neonicotinoid. Direct in vivo evidence from closely related neonicotinoids thiacloprid and acetamiprid shows clear brain penetration after systemic administration, supporting meaningful CNS access for the query.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct imidacloprid-specific brain/plasma or CSF measurement is present in the supplied evidence, but the close structural and mechanistic similarity to thiacloprid and acetamiprid provides strong transferable evidence.",
    "Quantitative unbound brain-to-plasma ratios and efflux-transporter data are not available, though the observed brain exposure in analogues indicates adequate CNS access."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

